ew":"The Rams are 4-4 through week 8 of the 2026 season and have outscored opponents 194 to 168. Both sides of the ball have been strong, with the offense 10th in EPA per play and the defense 3rd in EPA allowed. What sets them apart is the 2nd highest early-down pass rate in the league.","offense":"The Rams offense has been well above average by EPA per play so far. Their run and pass mix has stayed close to what game situations call for. Their biggest outliers in how they line up are the lowest RPO rate and a pre-snap motion rate that was among the highest in the league. Matthew Stafford has run the passing game and leaned heavily on Puka Nacua, who has drawn 25% of the targets.","defense":"The Rams have one of the best defenses in the league this season, ranking 7th or better in all five main efficiency measures. Its strongest area has been EPA allowed per play, where it ranks 3rd, and its weakest has been sack rate, where it ranks 28th. Byron Young has led the way with 4.5 sacks. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is an eight man box rate that ranks 12th highest in the league.","special":"The Rams have been about average in how often they go for it on fourth down. Their kickers have made 64% of field goals, which is the worst in the league. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the secondary, about 24% of the money on the books. Their offensive line was among the oldest in the league.","players":"Matthew Stafford has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 2,146 yards. Puka Nacua has been the clear top target with 25% of the looks and 565 receiving yards. Kyren Williams has handled most of the work in the backfield with 57% of the carries. On defense, Byron Young leads the team with 4.5 sacks and Quentin Lake has picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"Sean McVay is calling the offense as head coach and Chris Shula is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2022 Vikings under Kevin O'Connell and the 2024 Texans under Bobby Slowik. Their defense looks most like the 2024 Dolphins and the 2024 Bengals under Lou Anarumo.","trends":"The offense went from 2nd in EPA per play in 2025 to 10th so far this season, a big step back. The defense moved from 9th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 3rd so far this season, a modest improvement. The biggest change in approach was fourth down go rate, which went from 58% to 30%.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}